Diag.Reboot.Message=This PC will restart now.\n\nSave anything you are editing first. A restart closes every program on this machine, including the ones running in the twin.\n\nRestart now?
Diag.Reboot.Comment=Desktop Twin: restart after diagnostics fix
Diag.FixHint.RdpDisabled=This turns on the Remote Desktop listener on this PC (allowing others to connect over the network) and restarts the Remote Desktop service. If a twin or remote session is running right now, the fix is refused.
Diag.FixHint.RdpWrapper=This restores TermService to the stock termsrv.dll and disables the patch's auto-update task. Afterwards concurrent multi-user Remote Desktop stops working and you would have to reinstall the patch tool to get it back.
Diag.FixHint.TermServiceDisabled=This changes the Remote Desktop service start type from Disabled back to Manual and starts it.
Diag.FixHint.TermServiceStopped=This starts the Remote Desktop service.
Diag.FixHint.HelloOnly=This turns off "Require Windows Hello sign-in". You have to sign out or restart yourself for it to take effect.
Diag.FixHint.Default=This changes system settings.
Check.SummaryEntry=[{0}]
Check.NoIssues=No problems found.
Check.Unknown=Unknown
Check.NotSet=Not set
Check.ReadFailed=Read failed
Check.None=None
Check.Home.Title=This system is a Home edition
Check.Home.Summary=Home edition has no Remote Desktop host, so the twin is "unsupported" here
Check.Home.Detail=This system is {0} (Home edition). Microsoft's documentation states that Home edition does not include the Remote Desktop host, so Desktop Twin is in an "unsupported" state on it.\nIn practice most Home edition systems can still create a twin session, so this does not block anything. If startup never connects, consider upgrading.\n\nHow to switch to Pro (only changes the edition, **it does not activate Windows**; activation is up to you):\n1. Disconnect from the network;\n2. Open Settings, search for "Activation", click "Change product key";\n3. Enter the generic key VK7JG-NPHTM-C97JM-9MPGT-3V66T;\n4. Confirm and restart; after the restart the system is Pro.\n\nYou can also enter a product key you obtained elsewhere; the result is the same.
Check.OldSystem.Title=Windows is too old
Check.OldSystem.Summary=This Windows has no Child Session API, so the twin cannot be created
Check.OldSystem.Detail=This app relies on WTSEnableChildSessions from wtsapi32.dll, which exists since Windows 10 1803. The export is missing on this system, so Desktop Twin cannot be used.\nPlease upgrade to Windows 10 1803 or later.
Check.TermDisabled.Title=TermService is disabled
Check.TermDisabled.Summary=The Remote Desktop service is set to Disabled, so the twin cannot be created
Check.TermDisabled.Detail=The Remote Desktop service (TermService) has the start type Disabled, so Windows never starts it and the twin cannot create a session.\nThe fix sets the start type back to Manual and starts the service. This does not make it start at boot; it only allows Windows to start it on demand, which is the default Windows behaviour.
Check.TermStopped.Title=TermService is not running
Check.TermStopped.Summary=The Remote Desktop service is stopped
Check.TermStopped.Detail=The Remote Desktop service (TermService) is not running, so the twin cannot create a session.\nThe fix simply starts the service.
Check.RdpOff.Title=Remote Desktop host is turned off
Check.RdpOff.Summary=fDenyTSConnections = 1, so this PC does not listen for RDP
Check.RdpOff.Detail.Policy=fDenyTSConnections = 1 (set by group policy), so the RDP listener never starts and the twin cannot create a session.\n\nThis value comes from group policy; editing the local registry would be overwritten, so the app cannot fix it automatically.\nEnable "Allow users to connect remotely by using Remote Desktop Services" in gpedit.msc under Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → Windows Components → Remote Desktop Services → Remote Desktop Session Host → Connections, or contact your domain administrator.
Check.RdpOff.Detail.Local=fDenyTSConnections = 1 (local setting), so the RDP listener never starts and the twin cannot create a session.\n\nClick "Fix" on the right to set it back to 0 and restart the Remote Desktop service.\nNote: this allows other machines to connect to this PC over the network, so it loosens a security setting. Restarting the service also drops every current Remote Desktop session, including a running twin.
Check.HelloOnly.Title=Only Windows Hello sign-in is allowed
Check.HelloOnly.Summary=The Microsoft account has no usable password credential on this PC
Check.HelloOnly.Detail="For improved security, only allow Windows Hello sign-in for Microsoft accounts on this device" is enabled. A Microsoft account then has no usable password credential on this PC, so the twin keeps reporting invalid credentials no matter what password you enter.\n\nClick "Fix" on the right to turn that switch off, but **you must sign out or restart for it to take effect**; the credential provider is chosen at sign-in, so it does not refresh by itself.\nYou can also turn the same option off manually under Settings → Accounts → Sign-in options.
Check.Wrapper.Title=TermService is taken over by a third-party patch
Check.Wrapper.Summary=ServiceDll points at {0}, which conflicts with the twin
Check.Wrapper.Detail=The ServiceDll of TermService points at a non-stock DLL (currently: {0}). Tools such as RDP Wrapper and SuperRDP unlock concurrent multi-user Remote Desktop by replacing that value, while Desktop Twin uses the Child Session feature of the stock termsrv.dll. One value can only point at one DLL, so the two are mutually exclusive.\n\nThe fix does three things: back up the current value, restore the stock termsrv.dll, disable the "RDP Wrapper Autoupdate" scheduled task (otherwise it writes the patch back at boot), then restart the Remote Desktop service.\nThe trade-off: concurrent multi-user Remote Desktop stops working and you would have to reinstall the patch tool to get it back.
